>
> Are you nuts?
>
>> be prepared, and don't do that with too few HPs.
>
> Exactly.  So, not very early in the game, then.

You don't want to chase unicorns until a couple of levels after mine
town if you do the mines first, until mine town if you do Sokoban
first. But after that chasing cross-aligned unicorns for the horn is
very worth the effort.

> I generally start thinking of it as being mid-game when I start
> spending more time on preparation for the future than on
> immediate survival.  In the early game, you're mostly just
> trying not to get killed right away.

Most folks define the early game as the mines plus Sokoban plus the
levels up to them, whichever order you do those two branches.  It is
useful to consider that context.

>> and to buy gear that keeps you alive
>
> Most of that is FAR more likely to be picked off monster corpses,
> in my experience, or out of chests, or just plain off the dungeon
> floor, than found in a shop.  There are  exceptions (like, I recently
> found an early hardware store that supplied a magic whistle,
> woohoo, and I think also a key), but in my experience most of
> the stuff in shops is not worth buying until you can reliably
> identify it -- some of it not even then.  I can't count the number
> of times I've found a shop that did not contain a single item I
> could actually use.

The problem with items from monster inventories is they fit a specific
menu so a lot of useful items are missing.  The advantage of shops is
they have entirely random items in their class.  Mine town with it's
tool and lighting shop makes up for lack of early shops in some games.

>> Very much what I suggested in my posting as well; don't
>> even get burdened, that's the best.
>
> I try not to stay burdened for any length of time.  The thing that
> most commonly causes me to be burdened is the need to carry
> a pick-axe to some particular place where I need to dig for some
> reason, e.g., to get around an unfortunately-placed trap.

I think you're carrying too many other heavy items.  Many of my
characters carry a pickaxe most of the game and I am unburdened almost
all of the time that I explore a new level.

> I suppose it depends on your role and race and alignment.
> If you were a lawful dwarf, you'd probably go into the mines right
> away.  If you're neutral or chaotic and human, however, trying to
> make a beeline for minetown is in my experience thus far usually
> a one-way ticket to the graveyard.  The mines are too dark and
> too open and have dangerously many groups of gnomes.

Some people like to do Sokoban first others the mines first.  It's
personal preference.  Once you learn to cycle up and down the stairs
until the main crowd on a level is cleared surviving to mine town is
straght forward.

>> > by which time my pet is often strong enough to kill
>> > him.
>
>> This sounds wrong; do you delay visiting mine town that late,
>> or have you regularly guaranteed means to polymorph your pet
>> to something a lot more powerful than a house-cat or a large
>> dog?
>
> I did have that one game where Slinky was polymorphed into
> a barbed devil very early...

A pet in mine town should be weak enough to not attack shop keepers or
the priest.  A pet on the way back up will usually take out the entire
watch.  I lose pets to the watch captain's sword in some games where I
allowed it to advance too fast going down the first time.

> But in general it's probably that I usually stay out of the mines
> until I feel confident that getting jumped by a gnome with a
> crossbow, two gnomes with arrows, a kobold with darts, two
> gnomes with thonged clubs, a gnome with a wand, and a
> dwarf with a mattock, simultaneously, in the dark, won't be
> immediately fatal.  Usually I wait until I've cleared at least
> three levels of Sokoban, so I know whether I'm going to get
> a bag of holding or not.

Check.  You need to learn to cycle up and down until the initial crowd
is thinned out on each level.

> Also, a large dog can certainly kill a shopkeeper.  I've seen
> that happen in more than one game already.

I've had a pet horse attack the mine town priest even.  Goodbye pet
horse.

> I've been placing high priority on getting some armor
> and a weapon as soon as possible.  Is that a mistake?

Depends on the weight of the armor item.  I tend to skip plate, banded
or splint armor because it's too heavy for the early game so I tend to
try on every blessed and uncursed armor item I managed to bring to the
mine town altar.  The result is pretty good AC without a very heavy
armor item.

> Should I be leaving that mail and cloak laying on the
> floor and running around in just a Hawaiian shirt?

There are many other armor items to consider.  Especiallya good MC#3
cloak.

> Should I be leaving the magic items (scrolls and
> potions and wands) all laying on the ground?

Wands are too dangerous.  Leave them in a stash one level up or even
better in a container.  The rest I tend to put in the per-level stash
then shuttle down in my main stash until ready to use them.

>> >> A tinning kit
>
>> > Ditto.  Also, it weighs more than almost any weapon
>> > and almost as much as the lighter pieces of armor, so
>> > it is difficult to imagine an early-game character from a
>> > non-strong role lugging it around prior to obtaining a bag
>> > of holding (or gauntlets of power).
>
>> If only for getting poison resistance, it's worth to carry it
>> with you.

When I find a tinning kit I either carry it with me or drop it with the
food near the down stairs.  Then I rush for it when I kill a monster
whose can might give me poison resistance.

>> Poison attacks can be an instant killer, mind. And
>> if it doesn't kill you it will reduce your strength and *then*
>> making your carrying capacity a Real Problem.
>
> Oh.  Oh.  You know, I bet that's been happening to me
> and I didn't notice or realize the cause.  I'm going to have
> to pay more attention to that.

Lost strength is a major problem.  Notice how much inventory control
work you need to do.  Learn what monsters are worth eating because they
might give poison resistance and don't eat any of the other poisonous
ones unless tinned.  A lot of your other problems will stop once you do
that.


